<article class="zzz"><header><div class="meta case-meta"><span class="case-meta__client-full">Procter &amp; Gamble / ZzzQuil</span><span class="case-meta__client-short">ZzzQuil</span><span class="case-meta__highlight">Sleep-first UX</span><span class="case-meta__role">Senior UX Strategist</span></div><div class="intro"><div><p>Product Strategy &amp; Mobile UX</p><h1>ZzzQuil<br /><span class="heading-accent">Sleep</span><br />App</h1></div><p>ZzzQuil’s familiar purple palette needed a different treatment on a screen used at bedtime. I led the UX strategy for the Sleepmate app and companion shopping experience, adapting the brand for two different tasks.</p></div></header>
  <CaseContents links={[{"href": "#case-section-1", "label": "Research"}, {"href": "#case-section-2", "label": "Visual system"}, {"href": "#case-section-3", "label": "Experience"}, {"href": "#case-section-4", "label": "Results"}]} /><figure class="hero"><img src="/images/zzzquil_hero.webp" alt="Person asleep in calming purple bedroom light" loading="eager" decoding="async" /></figure>
<section class="research" id="case-section-1" tabindex="-1"><div><p>01 / The context</p><h2>An app for bedtime.<br />A site for shopping.</h2></div><div class="copy"><p>ChrowmDesigns worked through agency partner Pocket Made on the Sleepmate app and companion e-commerce website. As Senior UX Strategist, I was responsible for the color system and information architecture, along with interaction design across both products.</p><p>The app brought together sleep content, sound mixing, and bedtime settings. The website helped people explore and purchase products. Mapping the routes between them exposed two important handoffs: downloading the app after a purchase and returning to the site to replenish a product.</p></div><div class="research__images"><figure><img src="/images/zzzquil01.webp" alt="ZzzQuil desktop site in bedroom context" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><figure><img src="/images/zzzquil02.webp" alt="ZzzQuil mobile sleep app" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><figure><img src="/images/zzzquil03.webp" alt="ZzzQuil responsive website system" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure></div></section>
<section class="zzz-detail" aria-labelledby="zzz-research-heading"><div><p class="zzz-detail__label">Research &amp; discovery</p><h2 id="zzz-research-heading">What belonged on<br />the screen?</h2></div><div><p>I reviewed contrast ratios and compared the existing palette with dark-mode accessibility requirements. An audit of 14 sleep and wellness apps examined how their interfaces handled brand expression alongside content.</p><p>The useful distinction was between the imagery and the controls. Photography and illustration could carry the brand, while navigation and background surfaces could be more restrained. That gave me a practical direction for adapting ZzzQuil’s retail identity.</p></div></section>
<section class="palette" id="case-section-2" tabindex="-1"><div class="palette__head"><p>02 / A new visual language</p><h2>Making the purple<br /><span class="heading-accent">work on screen.</span></h2></div><div class="copy"><p>I adjusted the purple palette’s saturation and luminance for screen use. Inverting the existing colors would have left the underlying hierarchy unresolved. The goal was to retain recognition while keeping controls and content readable against darker surfaces.</p><p>Gradients drawn from the product’s liquid form became background treatments. Photography carried more of the brand expression, leaving the interface surfaces quieter. P&G’s brand team collaborated on photography direction and product imagery; I reviewed that work against the interface system.</p></div><div class="palette__images"><figure><img src="/images/zzzquil04.webp" alt="ZzzQuil product image and restful palette" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><figure><img src="/images/zzzquil05.webp" alt="Lavender visual language reference" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><figure><img src="/images/zzzquil06.webp" alt="ZzzQuil product visual language" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure></div></section>
<section class="platform" id="case-section-3" tabindex="-1"><div><p>03 / Content first</p><h2>Platform Architecture &amp;<br />Design System</h2></div><figure><img src="/images/zzzquil_phones-01.webp" alt="ZzzQuil sleep app screens" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><div class="copy"><p>Navigation receded as users moved into content. The soundscape mixer offered separate volume controls for layered ambient sounds, so people could adjust a mix without leaving the listening experience.</p><p>A visual clock handled bedtime and wake time, with sleep duration calculated automatically. Notification preferences and more detailed settings remained available without crowding the initial setup. These decisions kept common tasks accessible while leaving room for configuration.</p></div></section>
<section class="results" id="case-section-4" tabindex="-1"><div><p>04 / Results</p><h2>Results &amp;<br />Usage Patterns</h2></div><div class="numbers"><div><b>#1</b><span>soundscape mixer became most-used feature</span></div><div><b>AA</b><span>WCAG 2.1 design target</span></div><div><b>3</b><span>platforms in one system</span></div></div><div class="copy"><p>The existing project record identifies the soundscape mixer as the most-used feature within 30 days of launch. That observation supports reviewing how people found and used it, but it does not establish that navigation placement alone caused its popularity.</p><p>The shared component system covered iOS, Android, and responsive web. Documentation described component behavior and visual rules so the development team could extend the experience without revisiting every design decision.</p></div></section><section class="zzz-detail" aria-labelledby="zzz-handoff-heading"><div><p class="zzz-detail__label">Handoff &amp; reflection</p><h2 id="zzz-handoff-heading">Keep the system useful<br />after delivery.</h2></div><div><p>The component guidance covered buttons and cards as well as typography and interaction states. Each platform had its own constraints, so consistency meant shared rules rather than identical screens.</p><p>I would add usability sessions in participants’ actual bedtime routines. Reviewing a screen during a daytime call leaves out the setting it was designed for. Testing in that context would give better evidence for the next round of color and interaction decisions.</p></div></section></article>
